hi everyone eddie wu here it's a real  privilege to be speaking to you as part  of national eos  awareness week eos awareness week is all  about helping our community  understand what eosinophilic disorders  are and appreciate what it's like to  live with them and the struggles that  that can mean  my family is affected by eosinophilic  disorders my son has  eosinophilic esophagitis and so i was  really grateful for the help of  communities  like aussiee which was started by sarah  gray  in response to the fact that there was  so little information out there about  this very  rare set of disorders and so as part of  this week what i want to read to you  is a book that sarah wrote called meet  arabella  it's something which i found enormously  helpful to know that we weren't alone  and to wrap our head around the  particular struggles that our son was  going through  so it's amazingly the 10th anniversary  of this book and you can get a copy of  this at the aussie  website it's really wonderful to get a  better sense  of what the people around us are  experiencing especially when some of the  struggles  can be really invisible and they often  affect us socially  so i'm going to read this book now and i  hope you enjoy  this is meet arabella by sarah gray  arabella is just like every other kid in  her class  except sometimes she gets bubbles in her  throat  and rumbles in her tummy it is not  unusual for arabella to get bubbles in  her throat and rumbles in her tummy  because she has been getting those  feelings for as long  as she can remember  it all started when arabella was a baby  she was just like  every other baby in her play group  except when arabella ate food  it didn't make her feel good it made her  feel  sick sometimes she got a tummy ache  sometimes she had runny poo and  sometimes  she found it hard to swallow her food  arabella's mum and dad did not know what  was making her feel that way  so they took her to the doctor the  doctor did not know what was making  arabella feel that way  either so he said she needed to have  some tests  one of the tests arabella had to have  was a blood test  she tried to be very brave  but still arabella's doctor could not  find what was making her feel that way  he suggested that she go to a special  doctor called a pediatrician  the pediatrician thought about all the  things arabella was feeling  and said that she needed to see a  stomach doctor called  a gastroenterologist the  gastroenterologist  said she wanted to have a look inside  arabella's throat and tummy  with a special camera called an  endoscope  she hoped that the endoscopy would help  her to find out what was making arabella  feel that way  arabella was a little scared when she  went to the hospital for the endoscopy  but a very nice doctor called an  anesthetist helped her to go to sleep  when she woke up the endoscopy was all  over and she only had  a little sore throat  they finally knew what was making  arabella feel that way  the gastroenterologist said that she had  something called  isinophilic esophagitis her mum and dad  said  eozono what  so she explained that meant arabella had  too many white blood cells called  easenophiles in her throat and that  these blood cells  didn't like it when she ate certain  foods  she said most people called it eoa for  sure  now that all the doctors knew that  arabella had eoe  they could try to make her feel better  the doctor said arabella would feel  better if she drank a special type of  drink  called an elemental formula  the director explained that if arabella  did not drink enough of the formula to  grow big and strong  then she may need to have it through a  tube  the tube could be put down her nose into  her tummy  or straight into her tummy by something  called a peg  or a button the doctor  also wanted arabella to visit another  special doctor  called an allergist to help find out  what foods she may be allergic to  arabella was a little nervous when she  met the allergist for the allergy  testing  but it didn't hurt much at all and they  found out she was allergic to egg  dairy soy corn wheat  and fish so arabella  stopped eating those foods and she went  to talk to a dietitian  about the best foods for her to eat  that was five years ago and arabella  doesn't remember much of it very well  now she is seven years old every day  before school  she has to have her medicines and then  pack her special formula  to give her energy at school  sometimes her classmates help to remind  her to drink her formula  they know why she needs it because the  teacher explained to the kids about her  allergies  and her eozonophilic esophagitis  they all said iozino what  arabella is the only kid in her school  that has eoe  and sometimes this makes her feel  different  but one day arabella met a boy named  henry  who had eoe 2 and it made her feel good  to know someone  who was just like her do you know  someone  like arabella  and that's the end of the story i hope  you found that a lovely insight into the  world of children  and adults who suffer from easinophilic  disorders  um they can affect the esophagus or  anywhere along the gastrointestinal  tract  and one of the most challenging things  about it is that because it affects the  food that you eat and it's often  misunderstood it really has an impact on  every social interaction  you have so many times that we gather to  celebrate or be  joyful and enjoy each other's company is  when we eat  so sensitivity and understanding to that  is super important  and that's why i'm so grateful for this  story  i hope you have a great national eos  awareness week there are lots of other  fantastic videos  being released and i hope they all  enrich your understanding of these  conditions  and what we can do to help people who  suffer from them
 

